# Live Boot — packages installed in the live image.
#
# The Beerus console is pure Python on the standard library, so
# this list is short on purpose: the interpreter, the drive tools
# the engine runs, the live system itself, and a small operator
# toolkit for the spare consoles (copying job documents off the
# machine, inspecting media). Nothing here opens a network
# service; the destruction environment needs no network at all.

# The live system
live-boot
live-config
systemd
systemd-sysv

# Beerus itself
python3

# The tools the engine runs: drive listing, ATA Secure Erase,
# NVMe Sanitize / Format / sanitize status
util-linux
hdparm
nvme-cli

# Operator toolkit for the spare consoles (copying job documents
# off: mount + cp for a USB device, scp for a network drop)
coreutils
less
openssh-client

# The graphical session (Phase 4): the X server core with the
# generic framebuffer and VESA drivers (they run on hardware
# with no native driver, which is most machines a key is plugged
# into), libinput for keyboards and pointers, xinit for startx
# (the launcher's way in), the PySide6 Qt libraries the boot UI
# is built on, and DejaVu Sans — the font the boot UI's stack
# names for this image. The text console needs none of this:
# when a graphical session cannot start, the launcher falls
# back to the console, which is always available.
xserver-xorg-core
xserver-xorg-input-libinput
xserver-xorg-video-fbdev
xserver-xorg-video-vesa
xinit
python3-pyside6.qtcore
python3-pyside6.qtgui
python3-pyside6.qtwidgets
fonts-dejavu-core
